## Branches and releases

Use these branches in this repository:

- `main` holds stable releases. Each published release has a `vX.Y.Z` tag.
- `dev` holds the shared development version. New work starts here and returns here after review and tests.
- `feature/<short-name>` is one change started from `dev`.
- `experiment/<short-name>` is a trial method started from `dev`.
- Coding agents use `codex/<short-name>` or `codex/experiment/<short-name>` and follow the same process.

### Make a change

Check local work before switching branches. Start a feature or experiment from the current `dev`. Write its tests, code, documentation and examples. Open a pull request targeting `dev`.

{{List this repository's development check commands and review requirements.}}

### Release a tested version

Finish the version and release notes on `dev`. Run the release checks and review a pull request into `main`. Preserve shared history with a normal release merge. Tag the final `main` commit, then publish from that tag and merge `main` back into `dev`.

{{List required release checks, notebook checks, installed-package checks and external integrations. State who prepares the release.}}

If a temporary `release/X.Y.Z` branch is needed, start it from the chosen `dev` commit and accept release fixes only. Merge the released history back into `dev` after publication.

An urgent released-version fix starts on `hotfix/<short-name>` from `main`. Test and release that narrow fix, then bring it back into `dev`.

### Shared branch settings

Where supported, protect `main` and `dev` with pull requests and one required
`ci` check. Request another maintainer's review when available. Do not require
repeated updates after unrelated development merges. Do not force-push or delete
the shared branches. Keep `main` as the default and target ordinary PRs to `dev`.

Run one development job per PR revision on Python 3.13 (Node.js 22 for frontend
work). Cancel superseded PR runs. Do not duplicate the suite on feature pushes,
merge pushes or label changes. Run full release verification explicitly before
tagging or publishing, using the same immutable dependencies throughout.
Run affected integrations before merging changes to their behaviour.

Delete completed feature and `codex/` branches only after their commits are
preserved in `dev`. Preserve dirty worktrees and named ongoing experiments.
After a requested main synchronization, make `dev` and `main` point to the same
commit; this does not publish or deploy a release.

Stable describes the tested release and its stated scope. Experimental methods and scientific validation limits must remain clearly labelled. Never move or reuse a published tag.

